## 5. The Relation Surface

### 5.1 The typology is discovered, not declared

Given `d_f` and `d_s`, every ordered claim pair occupies a point in a two-dimensional space. Relation classes are **regions of that space**, and a class is *admitted* only where the measurement produces a populated, stable region.

| Region | Reading |
|---|---|
| `d_f` low both directions, `d_s` low | Not two claims. One claim, redundantly stored |
| `d_f` low both directions, `d_s` high | Same work, maximally different construction — *polar*. See §5.4 |
| `d_f` low one direction only | Directed coverage — one claim generalizes the other |
| `d_f` high, `d_s` mid | Different work, ambiguous structural contrast — *inverse* |
| `d_f` high, `d_s` high | Unrelated on both axes |

**The named classes are hypotheses about where populated regions will be found, not definitions.** If the measurement produces no stable population at `d_f` low / `d_s` high, polar opposition is not a real relation in this store and the name is withdrawn. If it produces a stable population somewhere unnamed, that region gets a name after it is characterized, never before.

The discipline is stated because the failure it guards against is specific and seductive: a two-axis space with three states per axis has nine cells, and naming all nine because the grid looks incomplete produces a taxonomy that predicts nothing.

### 5.2 Unproduced is not the same as nonexistent

v0.1 said a class exists only where the measurement produces a stable region. That is too strong, and the overreach is the same one the framework criticizes elsewhere: it treats the absence of a measurement as evidence about the world.

> **A class is *admitted* where the measurement produces it. A class may be *hypothesized as unreachable* where there is structural reason to expect a region and no available instrument reaches it.** The two are recorded differently and neither is silently converted into the other.

The distinction matters because instrument coverage is uneven by construction. `d_f` is measured by perturbation over a probe distribution, so regions of the claim space that probes rarely touch are measured badly — the same rare-knowledge bias the companion paper mitigates with an external annotation channel, arriving here as blind spots on the surface rather than as decaying claims.

An unreachable-but-hypothesized region is not usable and is not discarded. It is a stated expectation with a stated reason why no measurement supports it, which is the honest form and also the form that tells a later reader what instrument to build.

### 5.3 The surface stays two-dimensional

A proposed simplification collapses the two axes into a single index — some ratio of functional to structural distance — so pairs can be ranked on one number.

This must not be done, for the reason the companion paper does not use scalar confidence. A pair at (0.1, 0.1) and a pair at (0.8, 0.8) have the same ratio and are in entirely different situations: the first is two nearly identical claims, the second differs substantially on both axes. Collapsing the surface reintroduces exactly the failure the two-sided ledger was built to prevent, one level up.

### 5.4 The polar region is the epistemic-equivalence region

Two hypotheses are **epistemically equivalent** under a knowledge state when no information available to that state distinguishes them — formally, when the evidence is equally likely under both. Radically different underlying situations; identical predictive consequences given what is known.

That is `d_f` low with `d_s` high. Same work, different construction. **The polar region and the epistemic-equivalence class are the same region of the surface**, and this identification is the paper's most concrete result, because it converts a coordinate into a first-order instruction.

The companion paper disposes of such pairs by **non liquet**: the proceeding closes without a finding and neither claim's standing changes. That disposition currently fires on a relational fact nobody measures. Measuring it here supplies the trigger:

> **A pair in the polar region is routed to non liquet rather than adjudicated.** Adjudication between epistemically equivalent claims cannot produce a finding, so running it consumes governance compute to reach a foregone conclusion.

And one hard rule follows, which is worth stating because the tempting alternative is available and wrong:

> **A tie within an epistemic-equivalence class must not be broken by any internal measure.** Retrieval frequency, downstream reliance, corroboration count within the store, and every other quantity the system computes about itself are `τ_int`. Breaking the tie by consensus produces an answer that is well-formed, stable, and unrelated to which claim is true. The tie stays unbroken until `τ_ext` separates them.

This is the same prohibition the companion paper enforces at claim level with commitment drift, arriving here as a rule about a region rather than about a claim. It also gives the polar region a *use* rather than only a name, which §5.1's discipline requires of any admitted class.

### 5.5 Relations are scoped

Exclusion in the companion paper is domain-scoped: two claims cannot both hold *within domain D*. Relations inherit this. Two claims may be polar opposites in one domain and unrelated in another, and a relation asserted globally is a scope error.

Scope is derived from observed invocation rather than declared, for the reason the companion paper gives: declared scope is exploitable, since a claim can evade adjudication by acquiring a narrow self-serving domain. The cost is the same bias — the store resolves relations well among frequently co-retrieved claims and badly among rare ones.

### 5.6 The surface has no origin, and must be anchored

Both measures are **relative**. `d_f` is the divergence produced when one claim occupies another's position; `d_s` is the difference between two neighbourhood shapes. Each positions a pair against itself. Nothing measured so far establishes an absolute coordinate, which has two consequences that were not visible while the axes were treated as fixed scales.

