Worse: without an absolute reference the layer cannot reach beyond local proximity. Relative positioning works where a reference is at hand. It fails for wide separations, because there is nothing to position against — the same reason a system navigating by local relative coordinates handles adjacent steps effortlessly and cannot execute a long jump without looking. Applied here: resolution can compare pairs that already co-retrieve, because co-retrieval supplies the local reference, and it has no way to place a pair drawn from two distant regions. §9.4 recorded this as sampling bias against rare claims. It is not sampling bias. It is structural confinement to the neighbourhood, and no amount of additional measurement escapes it.
The repair is the one available to any system lacking an internal map: establish position by periodic reference to something fixed outside the measurement.
An anchor set is a fixed collection of claim pairs with independently established relations, re-measured every pass. Anchor pairs are chosen to span the surface — near-identical, polar, directed, unrelated — and to span the store's regions, including regions the live measurement rarely reaches. Measured coordinates are reported relative to the anchor set rather than to the live population.
Three things this supplies. Coordinates become comparable across stores and across passes, which is what makes replication meaningful and what makes drift in the measures themselves detectable — anchor coordinates moving while the anchors have not changed is an instrument fault, not a finding. Pairs from distant regions become placeable, since each can be positioned against anchors rather than against each other. And §6.3's instability check gains a reference: instability on anchor pairs is an instrument problem, instability on live pairs while anchors hold steady is a fact about the store.
The anchor set is authored, and therefore subject to the companion paper's warning that a constraint you designed is still a constraint you designed. It is not a source of truth about relations. It is a ruler, and the requirement on a ruler is that it not change while being used.
6. Relational Descriptions Are Claims
6.1 They carry ledgers
A relational description is an assertion that can be right or wrong, and it therefore requires the same treatment any assertion gets.
The argument is easiest to see in a case a naive design gets wrong. A taxonomy of relations needs a category for no significant relation on this axis. But that silently merges two states: we compared these thoroughly and found them unrelated, and we have never compared these. Those are gap and glut at the relational level, and the companion paper's founding move was refusing to conflate them.
So relational descriptions carry two-sided ledgers (e⁺, e⁻) exactly as claims do, with w and τ meaning what they mean there. A relation at low τ is unexamined. A relation at high τ with w ≈ 0 has been examined and is contested — a real and informative state, and the state in which two claims are genuinely ambiguous with respect to each other rather than merely unstudied.
This is where the two papers are established to share a spine. The evidence ledger ports to relational governance because gap and glut are facts about evidence, not about which limb is doing the work.
6.2 Resolution does not fill them
Resolution emits relational descriptions at (0,0). It does not weigh them.
Accumulating evidence on a relational description requires evidence about the underlying claims, which is governance's output, and combining the two is the conjunction the third paper treats. The direction of flow is fixed and asymmetric:
Evidence flows from claims to relations. It never flows from relations to claims.
A relation cannot corroborate a claim. If it could, resolution would be an evidence source with no external terminus, which §2.2 forbids.
6.3 A third state: measurement instability
Gap and glut are states of the evidence. There is a third state that belongs to the instrument, and conflating it with either produces a wrong response.
A relation is unstable when repeated measurement of the same pair, under the same conditions, yields different coordinates. This is neither a gap (a stable measurement has been taken; there simply is not evidence about the relation yet) nor a glut (the disagreement is between measurements, not between evidence).
The right response differs from both. A gap warrants investigation; a glut warrants scoping or non liquet; instability warrants withdrawing the measurement and investigating the instrument. Treating instability as a glut records a contested relation where none was established. Treating it as a gap invites re-measurement that will produce the same instability.
Instability is the relational form of the companion paper's highest-severity failure — claim instability, where the same nominal claim yields inconsistent estimates across probing methods. Here it is cheaper to detect, because relations can be re-measured on demand while claims must be probed by expensive methods. A store showing widespread relational instability has a claim-individuation problem that T0.1 may have missed at its chosen granularity.
6.4 A defect should be visible on both axes
An instrument check that costs nothing to state and is worth stating.
If two claims genuinely stand in a relation, the relation should be detectable on both axes — not with the same magnitude, but consistently in sign. A pair that reads as polar on the functional axis and as unrelated on the structural axis is either a real relation one instrument is missing, or an artifact of the instrument that found it.
Cross-axis agreement corroborates a relation; single-axis detection is a candidate, not a finding. Persistent single-axis detection concentrated in one region of the surface is a signal about the instrument rather than about the store, and is chargeable against the measurement configuration.
This does not require the axes to correlate globally — §9.3 requires the opposite. It requires that when a relation is present, both instruments register something. Global independence with local co-detection is exactly what two genuine access modes to one underlying property look like.
7. Authority
7.1 Resolution has positive authority, necessarily
The companion paper's governance layer is removal-only: it may suspend, refuse, decline to promote, void, and record, and it may not assert. That constraint is what makes the whole architecture bounded.
Resolution cannot have it. Producing a relational description is producing content that did not previously exist, and a component that only subtracts cannot compare. This is not a weakening of the principle — it is what distinguishes the two limbs. A generative structure assembled from constraining machinery is not a weak generative structure; it is not one.
The question is therefore not how to make resolution removal-only, but how to bound a component that necessarily asserts.
7.2 The bounding already exists
The companion paper contains exactly one operation with positive authority — abstractive synthesis — and works out that operation's bounding in detail. Abstraction is a resolution operation appearing inside the governance paper, which is why it sits uncomfortably there and resists the bounding technique the rest of that paper relies on.
Its bounding transfers directly:
Enter at (0,0). A synthesized product does not inherit the evidence of what it was synthesized from. A relational description likewise inherits nothing from the claims it relates.
No citation until promoted. Retrievable, but not usable as support until it has earned its own record.
Declared scope, scored by coverage. The product declares what it covers, and its evidence is measured against the declared scope rather than against its own distribution. Bounded from both directions: over-declaring is exposed by coverage measurement; under-declaring strips the generality that motivated the product.
Sources retained until coverage. What was summarized is kept until the summary's evidence covers its declared scope, so re-derivation stays possible.
7.3 The selection axis, and why resolution escapes what abstraction does not
The companion paper cannot fully bound abstraction: the content of a synthesized claim can be bounded, but its selection cannot, because the claim was drawn from an unbounded space of possible summaries and the alternatives were never enumerated.
Resolution does not have this problem, and the reason is §5.1.
A relational description is not chosen from an unbounded space. It is a coordinate computed from two measurements over a pair that already exists in the store. There is no alternative relation that might have been selected instead, because nothing was selected — the pair's position on the surface is determined by d_f and d_s, and the only discretion is in the measures themselves, which are fixed policy subject to the companion paper's transmutation and lineage rules.
This is what the discipline in §5.1 buys, and the cost of abandoning it is specific. A relation assigned from a taxonomy has an unbounded selection axis and inherits abstraction's unresolvable problem. A relation computed as a coordinate does not. The choice between a label set and a measurement surface determines whether the second limb can be bounded at all.
The bound depends on comparing only pairs that exist. If resolution were permitted to generate candidate pairs — to hypothesize claims that would stand in interesting relations to existing ones — the selection axis re-opens immediately and the bound is lost. That restriction is what makes the layer safe and it is also what caps its reach; §9.6 notes where the restriction becomes load-bearing at second order.
8. Individuation
8.1 The degenerate case is the important one
d_f low in both directions and d_s low is not a relation. It is the finding that two stored entries are one claim, and it is the answer to the companion paper's largest unsecured assumption.
That paper defines a claim as an individuable behavioral abstraction and provisionally operationalizes it as a cluster of entries co-activating above threshold, while acknowledging that whether such clusters are stable is untested and that the substrate is a specification without a constructor. Resolution is the constructor. Two entries are one claim when they do the same work in both directions and are built the same way, which is a measurement rather than a stipulation.
8.2 The circularity is a fixed point
Claims are defined by position in the dependency graph, and the graph connects claims. This is circular, and the circularity is productive rather than vicious: it is a fixed-point problem, solvable by iteration from a rough initial segmentation rather than by definition from first principles.
The loop's function is convergence of claim individuation by iteration. Governance supplies a rough initial segmentation; resolution refines it by comparison; the conjunction validates; the product returns as evidence rather than as constraint; repeat.
Two consequences. Coherence improvement is the observable, and individuation convergence is the mechanism — measuring coherence without measuring individuation stability measures a symptom. And a fixed-point iteration saturates, which supplies the discriminating prediction: genuine reorganization of a fixed corpus approaches a limit, while a self-agreement loop does not.
9. Open Problems
9.1 The structural measure is two components, not a measure
Exclusion density and symmetry order both survive the ledger-blanking constraint and neither is sufficient. What else is admissible is the paper's largest gap. Candidates worth examining: path-shape distributions in the local subgraph, edge-type composition without provenance weighting, and stable feature-composition identity if the underlying feature stability is ever established.
9.2 Whether asymmetry is fully informative
§3.3 establishes that relations are directed and that the asymmetry between B|A and A|B identifies generalization. Whether the full directed structure the companion paper's support edges require is recoverable from substitution asymmetry alone — or whether some directed relations are invisible to it — is untested.
9.3 The correspondence question is deferred
Whether the functional and structural axes pick out the same units is the first question requiring both limbs' output simultaneously, and it belongs to the third paper. It is not merely deferred for convenience: the cross-axis correlation is the measurable form of this limb's closure condition.
If structural individuation is predictable from functional individuation, resolution tells the conjunction nothing that governance had not already said — a channel transmitting with nothing crossing it. That is not the finding that resolution is unnecessary; it is the finding that resolution's channel does not close.
The prediction runs against the architecture, which is the right direction. This program requires the two axes to come apart.
One refinement, which the third paper should absorb. A binary reading of the cross-axis correlation is too coarse. Two instruments can access one underlying property and still each carry information the other does not, in which case correlation is moderate rather than either high or low, and the closure test belongs on the residual after accounting for the shared component rather than on the raw correlation. §6.4's local co-detection requirement and this global independence requirement are compatible precisely under that reading.
9.4 Locality, and what anchoring does not fix
Two related limitations, now distinguished.
Scope derivation from observed invocation means relations among rarely-retrieved claims are poorly resolved, and rare knowledge is often the knowledge that matters most. §5.2 records the result as blind spots on the surface. Whether the relational layer needs its own analogue of the companion paper's rarity floor is unexamined.
Structural confinement (§5.6) is the deeper version and is only partly repaired by anchoring. Anchors make distant pairs placeable; they do not make distant pairs proposed. Something still has to decide that a pair drawn from two unrelated regions is worth measuring, and nothing here does. Anchoring converts an impossibility into a cost, which is progress and is not a solution — and it pushes the problem into §9.5, where it becomes the same triage question.
9.5 Cost, now worse
Every measure here is pairwise, and §3.3's directedness doubles the space: the surface is over ordered pairs. Nothing above addresses which pairs are worth comparing. The companion paper's salience machinery is the obvious source of a triage function, but salience is defined over candidates and evidence, not over pairs, and the translation is not written.
9.6 Whether the second-order layer needs these axes or different ones
The third paper's second-order resolution layer compares relations to relations, and assumes the two axes transpose unchanged. Two reasons to doubt that, one of which resolves a problem that paper flags as blocking.
A third axis appears at second order that does not exist at first. Claims within a store share a domain by construction, so domain distance is not a free variable when comparing claims and does not need an axis. Relations do not share a domain: the relata of one relation may sit in an entirely different region from the relata of another. Domain distance is therefore an independent variable at second order — and it is not captured by structural distance, since two relations can have identical local shape in maximally distant regions.
It is also the variable that carries the most information there. Deep analogy is relational correspondence in the absence of domain correspondence, so high domain distance with low functional distance is precisely the interesting region, and a two-axis surface cannot express it.
